WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.780
Speaker 1: Let's start today with a very meaty,&nbsp;
substantial discussion about the American economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:15.900
Inflation has reached its lowest annual level in&nbsp;
two years, and the right is coming up with new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.820
ways to talk about how the economy is absolutely&nbsp;
terrible. And that's the question we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:26.280
to address with real numbers today. I recently&nbsp;
reported to you on on Monday, in fact, Monday of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:31.440
this week or three months ago. I don't remember.&nbsp;
No, it was two days ago. I told you about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:38.400
better than expected jobs numbers that came out&nbsp;
of April during the Biden administration. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.380
hesitate to give too much credit or blame on these&nbsp;
issues in general to presidents because they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.900
to do with so many broader macroeconomic trends.&nbsp;
But everybody wants to make everything political.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:54.720
But let's not get ahead of ourselves. Let's start&nbsp;
with inflation and then we'll move on from there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.500
Consumer prices in April rise at slowest&nbsp;
annual rate in two years. Reminder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:10.860
Often when we see the inflation numbers from&nbsp;
last month, they are actually the numbers for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:20.340
the 12 month period ending last month. So in May,&nbsp;
we get the April 22 to April 23 numbers. In June,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:27.120
we will get the May 22 to May 23 numbers. This is&nbsp;
like a trailing 12 month period, consumer prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.940
in April. This is Yahoo! Finance. Consumer prices&nbsp;
in April. April showed inflation pressures remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:40.740
elevated while headline prices rose at the slowest&nbsp;
annual rate since the period ending April 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.060
according to the latest data from the Bureau&nbsp;
of Labor Statistics, the Consumer Price Index&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:52.080
revealed headline inflation. Inflation rose 0.4%&nbsp;
over the last month and 4.9% over the prior year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:58.440
in April. Now reminder, inflation has been very&nbsp;
high, as you can see here. This is a chart dating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:06.840
back to 1970. We had this period culminating&nbsp;
in June of 2022 where inflation got very high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.360
as high as it's been going all the way back to&nbsp;
the early eighties and then exactly as predicted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:17.100
uncontroversial. It has been declining, declining,&nbsp;
declining. My prediction was, and there's nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:23.100
special about me, I'm not patting myself on the&nbsp;
back. I'm not special in any way. Just the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:30.180
things were looking, getting an outlook of what&nbsp;
we were seeing, you know, in the used car market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:35.880
in the grocery store, in real estate, just looking&nbsp;
a little bit, being reasonable adults. Most of us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:40.080
some children in the audience, my&nbsp;
sense was by the end of this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:47.580
we would be somewhere in the low fives, mid&nbsp;
fours, roughly when it comes to inflation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:53.400
which is still historically a little high, but&nbsp;
dramatically lower half of where we reached.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.380
So that's happening. And of course you will.&nbsp;
You're already seeing right wingers saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.640
David, these numbers are obviously fake. Have&nbsp;
you seen the price of eggs lately? And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.800
it's not just driven by the price of eggs. This&nbsp;
is a measure that we've talked about a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:13.680
times. It's composed of a number of factors.&nbsp;
But let's now ask a broader question. If we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.060
look at all of those different indicators that&nbsp;
I often tell you about, to answer the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.880
is the economy doing well by traditional measures&nbsp;
which don't capture every aspect of the economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:29.880
but they give us a picture what's going on with&nbsp;
inflation, How is GDP, where is unemployment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:34.980
how is consumer confidence? How's the stock market&nbsp;
doing? What about trade balance? You get a picture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:40.500
that is quite unremarkable in the sense of things&nbsp;
are pretty much okay. Now, does that mean we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:44.640
have inequality? No, we do. And we've had it&nbsp;
for a long time. Does this mean wages haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:51.480
been keeping up with inflation? Absolutely. Going&nbsp;
back well beyond Joe Biden. But the picture is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:04:02.160
a very scary picture. You look at GDP, GDP in Q4&nbsp;
of 2022 was up 2.6% and in Q1 of this year was up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:10.200
1.1%, 1.1%. Again, this is annualized. This isn't&nbsp;
an incredible number. We look for 2 to 3%, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.520
it's not a bad number either. We saw, of course,&nbsp;
this is a chart going back that includes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:22.260
pandemic. Q2 of 2020. We saw one of the largest&nbsp;
GDP decreases in in a very long time. We made a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:27.120
lot of that back in Q3. And then the numbers have&nbsp;
just sort of been like, okay, at the beginning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:34.560
2022, we actually saw some negative GDP growth,&nbsp;
then more positive in Q3 and Q4 of last year and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:40.560
then now a positive but not explosively exciting&nbsp;
numbers. So GDP is sort of like, okay, you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:46.500
at the unemployment rate and you see, of course,&nbsp;
this big spike here. This was the heart of COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:51.480
of the pandemic. And since then, unemployment&nbsp;
has declined, declined, declined much of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:57.720
under President Joe Biden. How much credit we give&nbsp;
him depends on who you are. But be that as it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:06.300
you see that the unemployment rate has now gotten&nbsp;
very low, as low as it got under Trump, as low as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:12.840
it's been, quite frankly, dating all the way back&nbsp;
to the late 1960s. So unemployment is very low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:20.100
huh? Doesn't look like a disaster. We then look&nbsp;
at consumer confidence. Consumer confidence is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:25.860
another indicator representing the confidence of&nbsp;
the average person in the American economy. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:32.400
you see that consumer confidence did decline a&nbsp;
little bit in April. Not much, but a little bit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:41.520
But it is historically at quite a high level.&nbsp;
Consumer confidence is a little bit below where it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:48.540
was at the height of the stock market rally while&nbsp;
Trump was president. But it is historically quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:54.120
high. And you the chart is on the screen and sort&nbsp;
of the numbers speak for themselves. Well, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.120 --> 00:06:01.500
about the stock market? These are S&amp;P 500 returns&nbsp;
going all the way back to 1930. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:08.160
this very. Clearly shows the boom bust cycle of&nbsp;
the American economy. And as you can see here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:16.800
we had 2022 last year, a very bad year for the&nbsp;
stock market. 2021 was quite good. That was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:26.400
again, a year under Joe Biden. The S&amp;P returning&nbsp;
27% in 2021, losing 19% in 2022. And so far this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:34.860
year, the S&amp;P 500 has returned more than 7% and&nbsp;
it's only meh. So really, no major red flag, big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:40.800
scary data from the stock market either. Things&nbsp;
are pretty okay. And then lastly, you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:47.280
the trade balance, which many folks on the right&nbsp;
often like to discuss, important to understand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:54.540
So trade balance means how much are we exporting&nbsp;
versus how much are we importing. The U.S. has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.540 --> 00:07:00.900
had consistent trade deficits for a long time.&nbsp;
This chart, let's see if we can go back further.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:09.660
Um, there we go. So if you go back to the mid&nbsp;
seventies, the U.S. has had high trade deficits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:15.960
and this is because we import a bunch of oil&nbsp;
and consumer products and consumer products.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:21.960
More recently, oil has not been the source of the&nbsp;
trade deficit that applies under Trump and Biden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:27.660
even though Trump likes to pretend that it was&nbsp;
only under Trump that we weren't having a deficit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:34.260
on oil. But it's under Biden as well. And as you&nbsp;
can see, if we look more recently, since early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:41.640
2022, the trade deficit has been declining. So,&nbsp;
again, a situation that is much bigger than Biden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:47.700
but nothing hugely concerning. So what is the&nbsp;
big picture with the economy? The big picture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:53.160
with the economy is that stepping outside of the&nbsp;
political realm without assigning credit or blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:59.340
to Joe Biden or Kamala Harris or Donald Trump or&nbsp;
Mike Pence, We just take a 20 year view and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.780
look at what happened under Trump and what's&nbsp;
been happening under Biden. And it is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:10.680
difficult to make the statistical case that we&nbsp;
are in a disastrous economy today. Could a major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:17.640
recession be coming? Of course, that's a reality&nbsp;
of the type of capitalist economy in which we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:25.080
live. That was true under Bush, Obama, Trump and&nbsp;
Biden. But are we in a disastrously perilous place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.460
right now that we can point to when it comes&nbsp;
to any of these indicators? The answer is no

